fix(touch): make bring-up tracing visible and bound every wait

The 0112 bit-bang boot enumerated the USB console and then went silent: no
bytes, no panic, no reset, and the port stayed up.  That is the signature of
the bring-up thread hanging before the USB event thread can drain the log ring
(the event thread shares the single CPU, so a spin inside kebab_touch_start()
keeps it from running), most likely on the first real TLMM access or on a
bit-bang wait that never completes.

Three changes make the next boot answer that question without another guess:

  * The first MMIO access is preceded by a 3 s blocking sleep.  delay() uses
    assert_wait/thread_block for millisecond intervals, so the USB event
    thread runs and the host has time to open the CDC-ACM port before any
    register is touched.  Without that window, phase lines queued just before
    a hang would never leave the 16 KiB ring.
  * Every phase prints through kt_trace(), which flushes the log ring
    synchronously (the same path the panic/KDP code uses), and the first TLMM
    read of each pin names the pin and absolute address *before* the read.
    A silent console after "reading pin 36 TLMM_CTL ..." then means the read
    itself hung, not that the driver never got there.
  * Every bit-bang wait is bounded: clock stretch is 1 ms (200 x 5 us) and
    prints on the first four failures, the TCM header retry drops from 100 to
    20 with a traced retry head, and the bit-bang start/address/data/read
    phases already report the failing phase and both line levels.

delay(KT_BB_HALF_US) still spins for 5 us because blocking for an I2C half
period is impossible; the fix is to keep those critical sections short and to
give the console its window before the first access.  The SOUTH TLMM tile
mapping from 0112 is unchanged.
